A||||8||mrfixit58||Need to change heater core -- Please help.||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||11-16-2001||04:43 PM||||I need to change my heater core this weekend, will I need to **BELCH** (excuse me) burp my cooling system or will everything just take car of itself?<P>Thanks for your help,<BR>Roy<P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||avengador1||11-16-2001||04:50 PM||||When I did mine it took care of itself. It also wasn't as hard of a job as I had anticipated. Just make sure you put some rags, newspapers, towels , bucket,etc. to catch what is going to drain into the car's passenger footwell.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||11-16-2001||05:06 PM||||Mine also seemed to take the change in stride. No cooling problems so far anyway.<BR>Just go slow and it's very simple. It will leak some so try to cover all the spots it may go.  <BR>Mine was dripping onto the passenger side and it takes a long time to dry out.<P>Gary||||reg||14
Z||000003||Brandon86SE||11-16-2001||05:09 PM||||Yup!  Heater core is like a half hour job!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  3 bolts if I remember to get the plastic shell off in the footwell.  A couple in the front to take off the windshield washer resovoir (trust me, take it off), and pull the 2 hoses going to it.  Leak a lot of coolant, put in the new one, put it back together.  It was very easy.  Fiero has the easiest heater core change I've ever seen.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||mrfixit58||11-16-2001||05:23 PM||||Thanks guys. I'll probably tackle it in the morning.<P>Roy||||reg||14
Z||000005||wkayl||11-17-2001||12:20 AM||||When refilling the cooling system, piont the nose of the car downhill if your driveway slopes or jack up the rear with a floor jack.  It helps.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cozmo||11-17-2001||08:43 AM||||Hey Roy,<BR>Funny... I have to do the same thing today to Debbes' GT.<BR>Watch out, the heater box can hold A LOT of coolant <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>BTW, how's the new GT doing?<P>------------------<BR><B>BobC</B><BR><B>'85 2M6 Targa</B><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> <BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B>||||reg||7
Z||000007||avengador1||11-17-2001||11:34 AM||||I forgot to mention, when I did mine I capped the hoses so they wouldn't leak. This saves you from having to refill also.I used the "T" from an old flush kit to do this. Just put both hoses together on the "T" and clamp lightly.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||11-17-2001||02:33 PM||||Here's MY method: <BR>1) pull windshield fluid reservoir, and move aside.<BR>2) Loosen top hose, and remove. JAM CORK IN IT!<BR>3) Plug the top fitting of the HC with something. <BR>4) Loosen lower hose, ram a cork (or something) in it too...<BR>5) Stuff rag into bottom fitting. Remove cork from top, and let the rag soak up.<BR>6) while it's doing that, pull apart the air-box. If yours was leaking, it'll be full of fluid. a towel is a good thing to save your carpets from further staining/stinking.<BR>7) Pull HC out. <BR>8) Find 2 suitable plugs that will fit. now, FILL THE NEW HC WITH COOLANT, until it brims over. Ram the corks (or wooden dowels wrapped in inner tube) into it, and install. Connect the bottom, THEN the top. IF you're quick, you won't spill much<BR>9) Reassemble, run car.<P>This way, there's no large air-bubbles in the system. If you develop problems (I didn't) then simply burp as per Ogre's instructions  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
